### Why `build:libs` comes first

Libraries in this workspace are consumed from their **build output**.
`tsconfig.json` maps each library's import name to `./dist/<lib>`, so the app
compiles against the library's real public API rather than reaching into its
source.

`ng serve` does not build workspace libraries. On a fresh clone, or after
changing a library's public surface, the app cannot resolve its imports until
`build:libs` has run at least once.

The `prestart` and `pretest` hooks run it for you. Two commands cannot be
hooked and need it run by hand:

- `ng serve <app>` invoked directly rather than through `npm start`
- `ng test` invoked directly rather than through `npm test`

Use `npm run watch:libs` while working across a library and its consumer.

### Theming

Every application's `styles.scss` imports the design system's tokens, and the
shell that ships with each app carries a theme toggle.

Two attributes on `<html>` decide everything:

| Attribute | Values | Set by |
| --------- | ------ | ------ |
| `data-theme` | `light`, `dark`, or absent | the toggle; absent means "follow the OS" |
| `data-palette` | a palette name | the toggle |

The stylesheet declares every combination up front, so switching either is an
attribute write: nothing re-renders, and no component has to know a theme
exists. The preference is stored per browser and re-applied by a small inline
script in `index.html` before the first paint — without it, every load starts in
the system scheme and flips a moment later.

Add a palette in two places — `src/styles/_ref.scss` and the `PALETTES` array
in `src/lib/theme/theme.ts` — and `npm run check:contrast` will check it, in
both modes, against every declared pairing. It also fails if you add it to only
one of them.
